{"duration_api_ms":89727,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"d740ced8-42fd-49ff-97fa-c06aa045361f","total_cost_usd":0.34826619999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":63351,"cache_read_input_tokens":57921,"output_tokens":8327,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2240},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":63351,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3055,"cache_read_input_tokens":57921,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5430,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5430},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":8327,"cacheReadInputTokens":57921,"cacheCreationInputTokens":63351,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.34826619999999997,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2240,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"requirements\":[{\"id\":\"cron-schedule\",\"requirement\":\"On node1, an Ansible-managed cron job runs /usr/local/bin/inventory-maintenance daily at 02:15\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Playbook using ansible.builtin.cron (cmd-c0133c20310e494e) was applied via ansible-playbook from control node2 (cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92, changed=1) and confirmed idempotent on rerun (cmd-ae4274ee93d74418, changed=0). `crontab -l -u root` on node1 shows the exact entry `15 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/inventory-maintenance >> /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log 2>&1` (cmd-3cc8370343a147d5), and the same entry persisted after a full node1 reboot (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c0133c20310e494e\",\"cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92\",\"cmd-3cc8370343a147d5\",\"cmd-ae4274ee93d74418\",\"cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6\"]},{\"id\":\"log-retention\",\"requirement\":\"Command output is retained in /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The cron job's job line redirects stdout/stderr to /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log (append mode). Functional verification by manually running the exact cron command line confirmed the log file is created/appended with the script's timestamped output (cmd-bca4c311af214eab), and the log content survived a node1 reboot (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6). Actual execution at the scheduled 02:15 time was not observed within the session window, which is expected/unavoidable.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c0133c20310e494e\",\"cmd-bca4c311af214eab\",\"cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6\"]},{\"id\":\"ansible-implementation\",\"requirement\":\"Implementation is done using Ansible, run from a control node against node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"After resolving RHEL7/Python interoperability issues, a working ansible-core 2.16.14 venv on node2 was used to ping and configure node1 via the ansible.builtin.cron module, with the resulting crontab entry directly verified on node1.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-0cf9838014cb4c74\",\"cmd-595f4f3e07b84e09\",\"cmd-c0133c20310e494e\",\"cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92\"]},{\"id\":\"node-restart-evidence\",\"requirement\":\"Independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered state on every managed node\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1: baseline boot_id 0d6209e2... with crond active/crontab/log present (cmd-5504088e2d8d4ac6), reboot issued (cmd-3ae7d1643e2845db), connection drop and timeouts observed (cmd-299e1f7a03ac4e97, cmd-0b2a151235d641a6), new boot_id 46b864cf... confirmed (cmd-f8b1d3d39d7d4231), and post-restart recovery of crond/crontab/log/script verified (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6). node2: baseline boot_id 416c5cf5... with ansible tooling/ssh key present (cmd-668aff5ec7a84dd9), reboot issued (cmd-ebeb453227ea4a42), timeout observed (cmd-a2ace3abc4fe47e7), new boot_id c1da0375... confirmed (cmd-c9229a86372242f2), and post-restart recovery confirmed by a successful idempotent playbook run against node1 (cmd-bf3126564ead4330). The fixed global boot-identity snapshots corroborate both transitions (executor_comparison boot-identity diffs for node1 and node2).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5504088e2d8d4ac6\",\"cmd-3ae7d1643e2845db\",\"cmd-299e1f7a03ac4e97\",\"cmd-0b2a151235d641a6\",\"cmd-f8b1d3d39d7d4231\",\"cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6\",\"cmd-668aff5ec7a84dd9\",\"cmd-ebeb453227ea4a42\",\"cmd-a2ace3abc4fe47e7\",\"cmd-c9229a86372242f2\",\"cmd-bf3126564ead4330\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Reaching a working Ansible connection to a RHEL7/Python2 target from an ansible-core 2.20 controller required a lengthy troubleshooting sequence with several state-changing steps, all of which had a clear causal role and were left in a reasonable end state. `sudo yum install -y python3` (cmd-ea9206a663aa4139) installed stock python3.6.8, which was later superseded functionally by rh-python38 and a lower-version ansible-core venv; it was not reverted, but it is a small, low-risk, standard-repo package addition rather than damaging residue. `subscription-manager repos --enable=rhel-server-rhscl-7-rpms` (cmd-21e0100fed5b4726) and `yum install -y rh-python38` (cmd-5bb9f6e13d394e53) were necessary prerequisites for a compatible interpreter and were kept enabled/installed, which is appropriate since they support the final working configuration. The SSH key trust setup (cmd-2fd5a1a7eed44d36, appending CLUSTER_SSH_PUBKEY to node1's authorized_keys) was a required, bounded, appropriately-scoped step. No destructive mutations, no failed/conflicting services, and no unsafe exposure were observed; the lifecycle snapshot diffs show only expected reboot-related churn (new boot ids, transient systemd-private tmp dirs, ephemeral socket PIDs) with no added listening ports or failed units in the after_executor package-health/failed-services observations.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-ea9206a663aa4139\",\"cmd-21e0100fed5b4726\",\"cmd-5bb9f6e13d394e53\",\"cmd-2fd5a1a7eed44d36\",\"cmd-9cf5c66f5b5b474f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes of the public task were satisfied: an Ansible playbook using the cron module was applied from control node2 to configure a root cron job on node1 that runs /usr/local/bin/inventory-maintenance daily at 02:15 and appends output to /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log, confirmed by direct crontab inspection, an idempotent rerun, and a manual functional test that populated the log. This configuration also survived a full node1 reboot. The independent node-restart evidence protocol was completed for both node1 and node2 with clear pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and confirmed recovered state (crond/crontab/log/script on node1; ansible tooling and a working idempotent playbook run on node2). Reaching this point required substantial RHEL7 Python/Ansible-compatibility troubleshooting, but the resulting state-changing actions were bounded, appropriately scoped, and left no damaging or insecure residue.\",\"limitations\":[\"Actual execution of the cron job at its scheduled 02:15 time was not observed in the trace; functional confirmation relies on a manual invocation of the identical command line plus the persisted crontab entry, which is a reasonable but indirect proxy.\",\"node1 package-health (yum) checks timed out in all three lifecycle snapshots (before_prepare, after_prepare, after_executor), so native package consistency could not be directly verified pre/post the various yum/subscription-manager mutations.\",\"The exact content of node2's inventory.ini at the moment of the final successful ping/playbook run (cmd-595f4f3e07b84e09) was not captured, so it is not fully certain which interpreter path (stock python3.6 vs rh-python38) was actually exercised for the final applied configuration, though the outcome (correct crontab entry) is directly verified regardless.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"cron-schedule","requirement":"On node1, an Ansible-managed cron job runs /usr/local/bin/inventory-maintenance daily at 02:15","status":"satisfied","summary":"Playbook using ansible.builtin.cron (cmd-c0133c20310e494e) was applied via ansible-playbook from control node2 (cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92, changed=1) and confirmed idempotent on rerun (cmd-ae4274ee93d74418, changed=0). `crontab -l -u root` on node1 shows the exact entry `15 2 * * * /usr/local/bin/inventory-maintenance >> /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log 2>&1` (cmd-3cc8370343a147d5), and the same entry persisted after a full node1 reboot (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6).","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0133c20310e494e","cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92","cmd-3cc8370343a147d5","cmd-ae4274ee93d74418","cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6"]},{"id":"log-retention","requirement":"Command output is retained in /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log","status":"satisfied","summary":"The cron job's job line redirects stdout/stderr to /var/log/inventory-maintenance.log (append mode). Functional verification by manually running the exact cron command line confirmed the log file is created/appended with the script's timestamped output (cmd-bca4c311af214eab), and the log content survived a node1 reboot (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6). Actual execution at the scheduled 02:15 time was not observed within the session window, which is expected/unavoidable.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0133c20310e494e","cmd-bca4c311af214eab","cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6"]},{"id":"ansible-implementation","requirement":"Implementation is done using Ansible, run from a control node against node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"After resolving RHEL7/Python interoperability issues, a working ansible-core 2.16.14 venv on node2 was used to ping and configure node1 via the ansible.builtin.cron module, with the resulting crontab entry directly verified on node1.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0cf9838014cb4c74","cmd-595f4f3e07b84e09","cmd-c0133c20310e494e","cmd-1b74721dfeb94c92"]},{"id":"node-restart-evidence","requirement":"Independent provider-wide restart-evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, restart, changed boot identity, and recovered state on every managed node","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1: baseline boot_id 0d6209e2... with crond active/crontab/log present (cmd-5504088e2d8d4ac6), reboot issued (cmd-3ae7d1643e2845db), connection drop and timeouts observed (cmd-299e1f7a03ac4e97, cmd-0b2a151235d641a6), new boot_id 46b864cf... confirmed (cmd-f8b1d3d39d7d4231), and post-restart recovery of crond/crontab/log/script verified (cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6). node2: baseline boot_id 416c5cf5... with ansible tooling/ssh key present (cmd-668aff5ec7a84dd9), reboot issued (cmd-ebeb453227ea4a42), timeout observed (cmd-a2ace3abc4fe47e7), new boot_id c1da0375... confirmed (cmd-c9229a86372242f2), and post-restart recovery confirmed by a successful idempotent playbook run against node1 (cmd-bf3126564ead4330). The fixed global boot-identity snapshots corroborate both transitions (executor_comparison boot-identity diffs for node1 and node2).","evidence_ids":["cmd-5504088e2d8d4ac6","cmd-3ae7d1643e2845db","cmd-299e1f7a03ac4e97","cmd-0b2a151235d641a6","cmd-f8b1d3d39d7d4231","cmd-e10abe523eaa46e6","cmd-668aff5ec7a84dd9","cmd-ebeb453227ea4a42","cmd-a2ace3abc4fe47e7","cmd-c9229a86372242f2","cmd-bf3126564ea